# Theme Reference — High-Impact Slides

## Design philosophy

Clean, warm, cohesive. Every slide should feel like it belongs to the same family.
No visual competition. The content speaks; the design supports it quietly.

**The test**: If you removed all the text, would the slides still look like one unified deck?
If yes, the design is working. If each slide looks different, something is wrong.

---

## Color palette

### Base (always used)

| Role | Color | Hex | Use |
|---|---|---|---|
| Slide background | Soft white | `#FAFAF8` | All slide backgrounds |
| Surface / card | Warm cream | `#F2EFE9` | Content blocks, callout boxes |
| Subtle surface | Sand | `#E8E3DA` | Dividers, table rows, secondary cards |
| Body text | Warm charcoal | `#2C2C2A` | All body text |
| Secondary text | Warm gray | `#7A7874` | Captions, labels, footnotes |
| Divider / border | Light warm gray | `#D6D1C8` | Lines, borders, separators |

### Accent (one per deck, used sparingly)

Pick ONE accent at the start of each deck based on the topic. Use it on:
- The single most important stat or number on a data slide
- The CTA button or call-to-action text
- One section divider or highlight bar (not on every slide)
- The hook slide background (optional, if using a colored cover)

**Never use the accent on body text, headers, or decorative elements.**

| Accent option | Hex | Best for |
|---|---|---|
| Terracotta | `#B85042` | Business pitch, marketing, energy |
| Slate blue | `#4A6580` | Analytics, data, finance, academic |
| Sage green | `#5C7A6B` | Sustainability, wellness, lifestyle |
| Dusty mauve | `#8A6070` | Creative, brand, consumer product |
| Deep amber | `#9C6B2E` | Premium, hospitality, food |

If none fits, default to **Terracotta** (`#B85042`).

### What to never do

- Never use two accent colors in the same deck
- Never use the accent color at full saturation on backgrounds (it becomes the dominant color)
- Never use bright primaries (pure red, pure blue, lime green) anywhere
- Never use white text on a light background — always warm charcoal on light surfaces
- Never use drop shadows or glows — they break the clean aesthetic

